<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Evita Capitana</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words for Eva Perón; anthem of the Female Peronist Party</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marcha Perón-Ibáñez (words by Alberto Marino)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1953</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Version calling for Perón's return from exile</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">after 1955</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hugo del Carril's recording</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1949</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Football chant</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (refrain)</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">March of the Barracas Juniors club (Juan Raimundo Streiff; words by &quot;El turco Mufarri&quot;)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 1920s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody and words of the verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Murga of La Boca</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 1920s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody of the refrain</span></li></ul></section>
